.TH HIBR 1 "" "hibr @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hibr \- a small, fast shell that runs the bash you already write
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hibr
.RB [ \-d
.IR level ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-\-agent ]
.RB [ \-\-checkfirst ]
.RB [ \-\-explain ]
.RB [ \-\-plan ]
.RI [ script
.RI [ arg ...]]
.br
.B hibr
.RI [ options ]
.B \-c
.I commands
.RI [ name
.RI [ arg ...]]
.br
.B hibr
.BR \-v " | " \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hibr
is a command interpreter that runs a large subset of bash: variables,
quoting, the compound commands, functions, pipelines, redirections,
here-documents, command and process substitution, arithmetic,
.BR [[ " " ]] ,
arrays, parameter expansion, traps and job control behave as in bash.
To that it adds nested ordered maps, JSON that keeps its types, regular
expression capture into
.BR M ,
functions with typed parameters, results returned without a fork
.RB ( ret
and
.IR x " := " f ),
declared command-line arguments, in-process text and array operations,
native TCP, UDP, TLS and Unix-domain sockets, and modules loaded on demand.
.PP
Where it differs from bash on purpose, the difference is recorded as a
decision under
.I docs/adr
in the source; the language guide lists them.
.PP
With no
.I script
and no
.BR \-c ,
.B hibr
reads commands from standard input. When standard input is a terminal it is
interactive: it reads
.IR ~/.hibrc ,
and offers line editing, history, completion and job control. Otherwise it
runs what it reads, each complete command as soon as it has been read.
.PP
A script, like bash's, runs a command at a time as it is read: a syntax
error late in a file stops it there, after the lines before it have run,
with status 2.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " commands"
Run
.I commands
and exit. The first argument after them, if any, becomes
.BR $0 ,
and the rest the positional parameters, as in bash.
.TP
.B \-n
Parse only: report the first syntax error, run nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-checkfirst
Parse the whole script,
.B \-c
text or standard input before running any of it, so one that does not parse
runs nothing. The same as
.B set \-o checkfirst
or
.BR "shopt \-s checkfirst" ,
which also applies it to files
.BR source d
afterwards.
.TP
.B \-\-agent
For a script a program runs rather than a person: each error is one line of
JSON on standard error (the message keyed by its level, the file, the line,
the source line, and a column for a syntax error);
.BR "set \-u" ,
strict expansion and
.B checkfirst
are on; a terminal on standard input is replaced by
.IR /dev/null ;
and
.B HIBR_TIMEOUT
bounds each foreground process. The same as
.BR "set \-o agent" .
.TP
.B \-\-explain
Run nothing: parse the script and name the common mistakes in it, one per
line with the line number and what to write instead. The rules live in the
.B lint
module. Status 0 when it found nothing, 1 when it found something, 2 for a
syntax error.
.TP
.B \-\-plan
A dry run: follow the script's own logic, but refuse every write outside a
scratch
.B TMPDIR
of the plan's own, every network connection, and every program not known to
only read, and list each refusal with its line on standard error. A refused
program fails with status 1 and no output, so a plan stops where the script
needed a result it could not have. Not a sandbox: a program on the read-only
list runs for real. Given with
.BR \-\-explain ,
.B \-\-explain
wins.
.TP
.BI \-d " level"
Log at
.IR level :
0 errors, 1 warnings (the default), 2 information, 3 debugging, 4 tracing.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and the module ABI, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of these options, and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HIBR_MODPATH
Directories, separated by colons, searched for modules before the installed
module directory. Not consulted when running as root, which loads modules
only from the installed directory.
.TP
.B HIBR_RC
The startup file an interactive shell reads, in place of
.IR ~/.hibrc .
.TP
.B HIBR_HISTFILE
Where an interactive shell keeps its history, in place of
.IR ~/.hibr_history .
.TP
.B HIBR_TIMEOUT
In agent mode, the seconds each foreground process may run: TERM when they
run out, KILL two seconds later, and status 124.
.TP
.B HIBR_TLS_INSECURE
Set to 1 to accept a TLS certificate that does not verify. Certificates are
verified by default.
.TP
.B HIBR_DEBUG
The log level, as
.B \-d
sets it.
.TP
.B PROMPT_FN
The name of a function whose result is the interactive prompt, in place of
.BR PS1 .
.TP
.BR PS1 ", " PS2 ", " PS3
The primary and continuation prompts, and the prompt
.B select
prints, as in bash.
.TP
.B HIBR
Set by the shell to the absolute path of the running binary, so a script can
run itself, or another script, under the same shell.
.TP
.B HIBR_VERSION
Set by the shell to its version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.hibrc
Read by an interactive shell when it starts.
.TP
.I ~/.hibr_history
The interactive history.
.TP
.I @MODDIR@
The installed modules, loaded with
.BR "mod load" ,
.B need
or on first use of a command a module provides.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/hibr/desktop.log
Errors from a running desktop, which are kept off its screen.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The status of the last command run, as in bash, with these of its own:
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A command failed; with
.BR \-\-explain ,
the script had something to explain.
.TP
.B 2
A syntax error, input that ended inside a command, a misused builtin, or a
typed function given an argument it refuses.
.TP
.B 124
A foreground process ran past
.B HIBR_TIMEOUT
in agent mode.
.TP
.B 126
A program was found but could not be run.
.TP
.B 127
A command was not found.
.TP
.B 128 + n
A command was ended by signal
.IR n .
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a script, check one before running it, and see what it would do:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hibr build.sh
hibr \-\-explain build.sh
hibr \-\-plan build.sh
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep a function's result without a fork, and read JSON with its types:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fn add(int a, int b) { ret $((a + b)); }
sum := add 2 3
json parse doc '{"meta":{"stars":42}}'
echo "$sum ${doc[meta][stars]}"
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR dash (1).
The guides that come with the source:
.I docs/language.md
(the language, and every deliberate difference from bash),
.I docs/builtins.md
(every builtin),
.I docs/llm.md
(the whole of it on one page),
.I docs/grammar.md
(the formal grammar).
Online at https://github.com/osakka/hibr.
